      "routePreference": "preferred",
      "explanationAfterStep": "The enol ether takes a proton on carbon: the ring oxygen lone pair stabilises the cation as an oxocarbenium ion. This is the same cation as in acetal formation from a carbonyl compound, reached from an alkene instead."
    },
    {
      "stepKey": "alcohol-adds",
      "stepKind": "polar",
      "toStateId": "protonated-acetal",
      "fromStateId": "oxocarbenium",
      "canonicalArrows": [
        "lp O11 -> σ O11-C2",
        "π C2-O1 -> lp O1"
      ],
      "routePreference": "preferred",
      "explanationAfterStep": "The alcohol adds to the oxocarbenium carbon. The carbon it bonds to becomes a new stereogenic centre, so a chiral alcohol gives a mixture of diastereomers: the main drawback of THP. The app does not check stereochemistry yet."
    },
    {
      "stepKey": "deprotonation",
      "stepKind": "polar",
      "toStateId": "products",
      "fromStateId": "protonated-acetal",
      "canonicalArrows": [
        "lp O20 -> σ O20-H12",
        "σ O11-H12 -> lp O11"
      ],
      "routePreference": "preferred",
      "explanationAfterStep": "Tosylate takes the proton back, so the acid is a catalyst. The product is an acetal: stable to base, organometallics and hydride reagents, but removed by aqueous acid or acidic methanol, by the reverse of these steps."
    }
  ],
